{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation landed for review as Gerrit change 2085.\n\nAdditional pinning suite, beyond those named in the description. `tests/unit/test_read_freshness_parity.py` monkeypatches `sync.run_git` BY STRING NAME, a seam Serena cannot see. Folding sync's timeout shim into the shared `gitutil.run_git_bounded` initially relocated that seam (ruff then removed the now-unused `run_git` import) and broke 14 tests with `AttributeError: module 'rebar._store.sync' has no attribute 'run_git'`. Fix: `run_git_bounded` takes an optional `runner`, and `sync._git` / `push._git` hand in THEIR OWN module-level `run_git` global, resolved at call time — so the timeout fold is still constructed in exactly one place while every `<module>.run_git` monkeypatch keeps intercepting. This is the review's advisory finding about patch targets, made concrete.\n\nTwo implementation notes that refine the plan without changing it:\n\n- `s3_doctor.py` is listed in Scope but needed no edit. It already imports `gitutil.run_git_write` and `gitutil.is_transient_object_read_error`, both of which are now registry lookups, so it inherits the consolidation through the shared seam rather than through a local change.\n- `UNMERGED` is the one kind with no PRE-EXISTING marker table: the condition was detected structurally (`git diff --diff-filter=U`) rather than by stderr. It is registered with git's own refusal strings so the vocabulary is complete and the table test covers it; the existing `_recover_from_unmerged` control flow is untouched.\n\nMutation evidence — every new test was checked by perturbing the live implementation and confirming a specific test goes RED, then restoring:\n- remove the transient retry from `sync._union_merge` -> RED\n- narrow push_recovery's retry back to TRANSPORT-only -> RED\n- drop the policy-decline subtraction from `is_non_fast_forward` -> RED\n- drop it from `is_transport_retriable` -> RED\n- change the CAS exit code from 128 to 129 -> RED\n- delete the lease non-CAS rows (the 4afc/ebee exclusions) -> RED\n- neuter the invalid-object lookup -> RED\n- change classify's default from FATAL to LOCK -> RED\n- delete the UNMERGED row -> RED\n- delete the `gh0` GitHub push-protection marker -> RED\n- make the guard's `# git-marker-ok:` escape stop requiring a reason -> RED\nBoth guards were additionally proven able to FAIL against synthetic trees (a forked marker table, a reintroduced timeout shim) and proven NOT to fire on comment/docstring prose or on the legitimate `\"git timed out after\"` marker row.\n\nTwo mutations initially SURVIVED and were fixed by strengthening the tests rather than the code: the two policy-decline subtractions are invisible at the `classify` level (the policy rule runs first in the PUSH rule order, so the verdict is the same either way). The subtraction is load-bearing at the PREDICATE level, which is what the push loop actually calls, so a dedicated test now asserts `_is_non_fast_forward` and `_is_transport_retriable` each answer False when a policy cause is named alongside the broad marker.\n\nGates: `make lint` (incl. the complexity ratchet: active=46 new=0 increased=0) and `make typecheck` clean; 653 tests pass across the pinning suites plus the new module. One harness error in `test_read_freshness_parity` was self-inflicted — my `git commit` moved the worktree HEAD while that suite was running and conftest guards against exactly that; it passes in isolation."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787381450175845001,"uuid":"7cff732c-241f-4a75-bfe3-96a282c95c08"}